    "title": "Skydiving Plane Crash in New Jersey Injures Fifteen",
    "text": "• Fifteen individuals were injured in a single-engine plane crash at Cross Keys Airport in Williamstown, New Jersey.\n• The incident involved a Cessna 208B aircraft and occurred around 5:30 p.m. on Wednesday.\n• All fifteen people on board the aircraft were transported to area hospitals.\n• Emergency personnel responded significantly to the scene, as indicated by aerial footage.\n• Cooper University Hospital is treating all fifteen patients with varying degrees of injuries.\n• Three victims are under evaluation, eight are being treated for less severe injuries, and four suffered minimal injuries.\n• The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) has launched an investigation into the crash.\n• Details about the cause of the incident are currently limited.\n• Cross Keys Airport directed inquiries to Skydive Cross Keys, the commercial skydiving operation based at the airport.\n• The FAA's investigation process includes on-scene investigation, evidence collection, component examination, medical examinations, analysis, and reporting.\n• Common causes of aircraft accidents include pilot error, mechanical failure, weather conditions, and operational factors.\n• The FAA's final report will be made public and may lead to safety recommendations and changes in aviation protocols.\n• The National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) may also review the FAA's findings and issue its own recommendations.",
